    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How do I get my FIRPTA withholding back?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "FIRPTA withholding is reclaimed by filing Form 1040-NR for the year of sale, reporting your actual gain and attaching Copy B of Form 8288-A. If the tax you owe is less than the amount withheld, the IRS refunds the difference. You need an ITIN to file."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Do I need an ITIN to get a FIRPTA refund?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "An ITIN is needed to get a FIRPTA refund if you do not have an SSN, because Form 1040-NR requires a US taxpayer number. Applying for the ITIN early, ideally before or at closing, avoids waiting weeks after the sale before your return can be processed."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is FIRPTA 15% of the profit or the sale price?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "FIRPTA withholding is generally 15% of the gross sale price, not 15% of your profit. That is why foreign sellers with a modest gain often have far more withheld than they actually owe, and can claim the excess back on Form 1040-NR."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can I reduce FIRPTA withholding before the sale closes?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "FIRPTA withholding can sometimes be reduced before closing by applying for a withholding certificate on Form 8288-B, based on your expected tax. Each seller needs an ITIN; Form W-7 can be filed together with Form 8288-B only when applying for the certificate and conditions are met. Start as soon as you decide to sell."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Do both joint owners need an ITIN for FIRPTA?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Both joint owners normally need an ITIN, because each foreign seller generally files their own Form 1040-NR to claim back their share of the withholding. Check that each owner's name appears correctly on the withholding paperwork before you file anything."
